JavaScript Code:
```javascript
function updateSun() {
  const currentDate = new Date().toLocaleTimeString();
  const currentTime = new Date(currentDate).getHours();

  if (currentTime >= 0 && currentTime < 6) {
    document.getElementById("sun").style.backgroundImage = "url('path/to/sun-yellow.png')";
  } else if (currentTime >= 6 && currentTime < 12) {
    document.getElementById("sun").style.backgroundImage = "url('path/to/sun-bright-yellow.png')";
  } else if (currentTime >= 12 && currentTime < 18) {
    document.getElementById("sun").style.backgroundImage = "url('path/to/sun-orange.png')";
  } else {
    document.getElementById("sun").style.backgroundImage = "url('path/to/sunset-red.png')";
  }
}
setInterval(updateSun, 3600);
```
This code will fetch the current time and check what hour it is, then set the background image of a div element called `#sun` accordingly. The URL paths in quotes should point to images representing different times of day. You would also need to add additional cases to cover other hours of the day.
To make sure this works properly, you might want to test it out on your local machine before deploying to a live server. Good luck!
